# The whole arc, from one ark to the next.
#
# **Sean, 2026-09-20**, naming what this prototype is for: *the core game loop: start with an ark ->
# develop a planet -> launch an ark.* **This is that loop and nothing else** - every other test here
# says one thing about one rule, and this one says the things join up.
#
# **What it adds is that what one rule leaves is what the next one needs.** Every piece is checked
# on its own already - and a suite of pieces can pass entire while the arc they are pieces of does
# not close, because no test of a piece ever looks at the next one.
#
# ## Two turns, and one turn is refused
#
# **The bound is labour.** The second turn spends four - metal, the energy extractor, the energy,
# and the launch - and two citizens make two. **So a turn has to pass for them to breed**, which is
# the only thing the first turn is for once the ark is down.
#
# **One turn was tried and refused**: *`work` removes {labor where:1 quantity:1} and nothing
# matched.* That is the floor, measured rather than reasoned.
#
# **It was four turns until 2026-09-20**, when everything made began arriving at full capability -
# Sean: *Generally i want things created with full capability.* A built extractor used to be idle
# until the turn restored it, so the energy could not be mined on the turn its extractor was built,
# and neither could the ark be launched on the turn the energy was mined. **The rule took two turns
# out of the arc**, which is the clearest measure of it there is.
#
# ## What each turn is for
#
# **Turn one is the landing and the food.** The ark becomes two extractors and two citizens, both of
# which may work at once. Two labours: one mines six food and the other is wasted, and the waste is
# the point - nothing eats at `upkeep`, because a citizen off an ark owes no meal yet, so all six
# are there for `breed` to spend. **Four citizens by the end of it**, and the two food left over go
# with everything else loose, because nothing here has a bin.
#
# **Turn two is the planet and the launch.** Four labours, all four spent: metal out of the ground,
# an energy extractor built from it, energy out of the ground, and an ark built from the three.
#
# ## What the end state says
#
# **Four citizens, no food, and every labour spent.** The settlement that built the ark is still
# there and hungry - a launch is not a departure, it is a thing built. **What happens to them next
# is the next turn's problem**, and nothing here pretends otherwise.
#
# **Nothing is stored.** Four metal and five energy lie loose and would go at the turn's end; the
# test stops before that, because what it is about is the arc rather than the housekeeping.
#
# **This test is brittle and that is correct.** Every number in it is a consequence of every rule's
# cost, so tuning any of them moves it - which is what an acceptance test for *the loop closes* is
# supposed to do.

{test name:an-ark-lands-a-planet-is-developed-and-an-ark-leaves}

{given}
{territory id:1 name:territory-1}
{place id:1 of:territory-1 layer:surface name:place-1}
{place id:2 of:territory-1 layer:orbit name:place-2}
{capacity of:deposit for:extractor what:resource per:place} -> 1
{deposit where:place-1 what:metal density:6} -> 1
{deposit where:place-1 what:food density:6} -> 1
{deposit where:place-1 what:energy density:6} -> 1
{ark where:place-2 moving:1 gathering:1} -> 1

{when}
{deploy where:place-2}
{toil where:place-1}
{work where:place-1 what:food}
{end-turn}
{toil where:place-1}
{work where:place-1 what:metal}
{build-extractor where:place-1 what:energy}
{work where:place-1 what:energy}
{launch where:place-1}

{then}
{territory id:1 name:territory-1}
{place id:1 of:territory-1 layer:surface name:place-1}
{place id:2 of:territory-1 layer:orbit name:place-2}
{capacity of:deposit for:extractor what:resource per:place} -> 1
{deposit where:place-1 what:metal density:6} -> 1
{deposit where:place-1 what:food density:6} -> 1
{deposit where:place-1 what:energy density:6} -> 1
{extractor where:place-1 what:metal working:0} -> 1
{extractor where:place-1 what:food working:1} -> 1
{extractor where:place-1 what:energy working:0} -> 1
{citizen where:place-1 hungry:1 bearing:1 laboring:0} -> 4
{metal where:place-1} -> 4
{energy where:place-1} -> 5
{ark where:place-2 moving:1 gathering:1} -> 1
